    "snippet": "---\ntitle: The Mentor Hall of Fame\ndate: 1999-08-27\n---\nThe Mentor Hall of FameARTISTS, WRITERS, PHOTOGRAPHERS, PUBLISHERS, NOVELISTS, AND POETS |\n\n|\n\nVirtually anyone can benefit from having a mentor. And most well-known, accomplished and successful artists can identify people in their lives who acted as mentors.\n\nThe following list of mentor pairs was compiled by Rey Carr from a variety of sources including autobiographies, biographies, newspaper articles, personal interviews, and diligent historical research. If you know of mentor pairs that ought to be added, we have provided an opportunity for you to submit the names and details: Add a Mentor Pair. |\n\n| ARTISTS, WRITERS, PHOTOGRAPHERS, PUBLISHERS, NOVELISTS, JOURNALISTS, AND POETS |\n\n- Luigi Capello was a mentor to Quebec painter Ozias Leduc (1864-1955).\n\n- Polish writer Isaac Leib Peretz (1852-1915) is credited for creating a modern Yiddish literature and was a proponent of the language as a place of Jewish cultural identity. His poems, plays, humorous sketches, and especially his short stories experimented with form and brought psychological realism to his characters. His role as mentor to a generation of Jewish writers in Warsaw ushered in a new literary era for the Yiddish language.\n\n- Canadian businessman, journalist and politician Joseph-Edouard Cauchon (1816-1885) was a mentor Canadian portrait and religious image artist Antoine Plamondon (1804-1895).\n\n- American writer, newspaper columnist and playwright George Ade (1866-1944); and American humourist, newspaper columnist and film actor Robert Benchley (1889-1945) were mentors to American humour essayist and screenwriter S.J. Perelman (1904-1979) who was named as a mentor by British-Irish comedian, writer, poet and actor Spike Milligan (1918-2002); and British film actor, comedian and impressionist Peter Sellers (1925-1980).\n\n- Best selling American author of more than 40 books and Pulitzer Prize winner James Michener (1907-1997) is a mentor to photographer and author of more than 30 books Robert Vavra.\n\n- When Canadian artist, art educator and member of the Group of Seven Lionel Lemoine Fitzgerald (1890-1956) was inducted into"
  },
